from pathlib import Path
from runpy import run_path
g=run_path(str(Path(__file__).with_name('author-tools-generator.py')))
L=g['lesson']; C=g['course']

def library(slug, intro, setup, docs, prefix, rows, suffix=''):
    records=[]
    for title,idea,code,result,task,warning,q,a,w in rows:
        records.append(L(title,idea,prefix+code+suffix,result,task,warning,q,a,w,language='python'))
    C(slug,intro,setup,docs,records)

library('numpy','NumPy stores and calculates with arrays of numbers. Learn shapes, positions, simple calculations and copies using tiny lists you can understand by hand.','Examples need Python and NumPy in a separate local environment. They are shown here with explanations; they do not run in this website\'s playground.','https://numpy.org/doc/stable/user/absolute_beginners.html','import numpy as np\n\n',[
('Make a number array','A NumPy array holds values with a shared data type. np.array builds one from a Python list.','scores = np.array([2, 4, 6])\nprint(scores.tolist())','The output is [2, 4, 6]. tolist turns the array back into a normal list for easy reading.','Add 8 to the input list.','Do not confuse an array with one single number.','How many values are in this array?','3','1'),
('Read the shape','Shape tells you the size of each dimension. A table-like array has a row count and a column count.','grid = np.array([[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]])\nprint(grid.shape)','The output is (2, 3): two rows, each with three columns.','Draw this array as a table on paper.','The first shape number is not always the total number of values.','What does shape (2, 3) mean?','2 rows and 3 columns','3 rows and 2 columns'),
('Choose a data type','A data type describes how values are stored. An explicit type avoids guessing when size or precision matters.','values = np.array([1, 2], dtype=np.float32)\nprint(values.dtype)','The output is float32. The values are stored as 32-bit floating-point numbers.','Print values.tolist to see the decimal values.','Floating-point values cannot represent every decimal fraction exactly.','Which type was requested?','float32','A text string type'),
('Index a position','An index selects a position. NumPy, like Python lists, starts counting at zero.','values = np.array([5, 7, 9])\nprint(values[1])','The output is 7, the second value.','Read the final value using index 2.','Index 3 is outside this three-value array.','Which value is at index 1?','7','5'),
('Slice a range','A slice selects a range. Its starting index is included and its stopping index is excluded.','values = np.array([2, 4, 6, 8])\nprint(values[1:3].tolist())','The output is [4, 6]. Positions 1 and 2 are selected; position 3 is left out.','Try the slice [:2].','Basic slicing often gives a view of the original data, not an independent copy.','Is the stop index included?','No','Yes'),
('Calculate element by element','Adding two arrays of the same shape adds matching positions. This is element-wise addition.','a = np.array([1, 2, 3])\nb = np.array([10, 20, 30])\nprint((a + b).tolist())','The output is [11, 22, 33]. Each position adds one value from a and the matching value from b.','Subtract b from a and predict each position.','Array addition is not the same as joining two Python lists.','What does a + b do here?','Adds matching values','Joins the arrays end to end'),
('Broadcast one value','Broadcasting lets some differently shaped values work together. A single number can be used for every item in an array.','scores = np.array([2, 4, 6])\nprint((scores + 1).tolist())','The output is [3, 5, 7]. NumPy adds 1 at each position.','Multiply each score by 2.','Not every pair of shapes can broadcast; their dimensions must follow NumPy\'s rules.','Where is 1 added?','To every value','Only the first value'),
('Sum along an axis','An axis is one direction through an array. For this two-dimensional table, axis 0 combines rows to produce a total for each column.','grid = np.array([[1, 2], [3, 4]])\nprint(grid.sum(axis=0).tolist())','The output is [4, 6]. Column one adds 1 and 3; column two adds 2 and 4.','Use axis=1 to total each row.','Draw the table before choosing an axis if the direction is confusing.','What does axis=0 produce here?','Column totals','Only the first row'),
('Reshape without changing the count','reshape changes the arrangement of values. The new shape must hold the same total number of values.','values = np.array([1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6])\nprint(values.reshape(2, 3).tolist())','The output is [[1, 2, 3], [4, 5, 6]]. Six values now occupy two rows of three.','Try shape (3, 2).','You cannot reshape six values into a full 2 by 2 table.','Which new shape can hold six values?','3 by 2','2 by 2'),
('Filter with a boolean mask','A boolean mask is a list of true-or-false choices. True positions are kept when used to select array values.','scores = np.array([3, 8, 5])\nmask = scores >= 5\nprint(scores[mask].tolist())','The output is [8, 5]. The comparison keeps scores of at least 5.','Change the rule to scores > 5.','Use element-wise logical operators carefully; normal Python and/or do not combine whole array masks.','Which scores are kept?','8 and 5','Only 3'),
('Make an independent copy','A copy owns separate data. It is useful when changing a result must not change the original array.','original = np.array([1, 2, 3])\ncopy = original.copy()\ncopy[0] = 99\nprint(original.tolist())','The original remains [1, 2, 3]. copy[0] changes only the copied data.','Compare this with a basic slice view in a separate experiment.','Assigning copy = original only gives the same array another name.','Which method creates an independent data copy?','copy()','Only assigning another variable name'),
('Summarise a small dataset','The mean is the total divided by the number of values. The minimum and maximum describe the range.','scores = np.array([2, 4, 6])\nprint(float(scores.mean()), int(scores.min()), int(scores.max()))','The output is 4.0 2 6. These three summaries describe the center and the two ends of the data.','Add an unusually high score and see how the mean changes.','One average can hide important differences between individual values.','What is the mean of 2, 4 and 6?','4','12'),
])

frame='import pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.DataFrame({\n    "name": ["Asha", "Ben", "Cara"],\n    "score": [8, 6, 9],\n    "team": ["red", "blue", "red"]\n})\n\n'
library('pandas','Pandas helps work with data arranged in rows and columns. Learn to select, filter, group and check a small fictional score table with clear Python examples.','Examples need Python and pandas in a separate environment. The people and scores are fictional. Do not use real learner records for practice.','https://pandas.pydata.org/docs/getting_started/intro_tutorials/',frame,[
('Build a DataFrame','A DataFrame is a table with labelled columns and rows. Each list supplied here becomes one column.','print(df.shape)','The output is (3, 3). There are three rows and three columns: name, score and team.','Add a fourth fictional learner to every column list.','All column lists must have compatible lengths when building this table.','What does the first shape number count?','Rows','Only text columns'),
('Select a column','Selecting one column by name returns a Series. A Series is a labelled one-dimensional set of values.','print(df["score"].tolist())','The output is [8, 6, 9]. Only the score column is selected.','Select the name column instead.','A misspelled column name raises an error rather than guessing your meaning.','Which column is selected?','score','team'),
('Read by label with loc','loc selects using row and column labels. This table has default row labels 0, 1 and 2.','print(df.loc[1, "name"])','The output is Ben. The row label is 1 and the column label is name.','Read Cara\'s score using loc.','A row label is not always the same as its position after the index changes.','What does loc use?','Labels','Only physical positions'),
('Read by position with iloc','iloc selects by zero-based positions. Its row and column numbers describe where values are placed.','print(df.iloc[0, 1])','The output is 8: the first row and second column.','Read the third row\'s first column.','Do not pass a column name to iloc when it expects a position.','Which cell is iloc[0, 1]?','First row, second column','Second row, first column'),
('Filter rows','A condition creates a true-or-false value for each row. Use that condition to keep matching rows.','passed = df[df["score"] >= 8]\nprint(passed["name"].tolist())','The output is [\'Asha\', \'Cara\']. Their scores meet the chosen threshold.','Change the threshold to 9.','Be explicit about whether the boundary value is included.','Who meets score >= 8?','Asha and Cara','Only Ben'),
('Add a calculated column','A new column can come from a calculation on an existing one. Matching rows keep their relationship.','df["bonus_score"] = df["score"] + 1\nprint(df["bonus_score"].tolist())','The output is [9, 7, 10]. One point is added to each row\'s score.','Add a column that doubles the original score.','Keep the original column when you still need the unmodified data.','How much is added to each score?','1','3'),
('Sort rows by a value','sort_values orders rows by a selected column. The other columns move with their row so names and scores stay matched.','ordered = df.sort_values("score")\nprint(ordered["name"].tolist())','The output is [\'Ben\', \'Asha\', \'Cara\'], from lowest to highest score.','Set ascending=False.','Do not sort each column separately; that breaks row relationships.','Why do all columns move together?','To preserve each record','To hide the scores'),
('Handle missing values','A missing value means data is absent. isna identifies missing entries; filling them requires a justified choice.','values = pd.Series([2.0, None, 6.0])\nprint(values.isna().tolist())','The output is [False, True, False]. The middle value is missing.','Count missing values before deciding what to do with them.','Do not replace every missing value with zero unless zero has the right meaning.','Which entry is missing?','The middle entry','Every entry'),
('Group and summarise','groupby gathers rows with the same key. An aggregation, such as mean, gives one summary for each group.','means = df.groupby("team")["score"].mean()\nprint(float(means.loc["red"]))','The result is 8.5. The red team has scores 8 and 9, whose average is 8.5.','Read the blue team\'s mean.','A small group\'s average can be unstable; keep its size in mind.','Which scores make the red mean?','8 and 9','8 and 6'),
('Join related tables','merge combines tables using shared keys. Check whether each key is unique before deciding how the join should behave.','teams = pd.DataFrame({"team": ["red", "blue"], "room": ["A", "B"]})\njoined = df.merge(teams, on="team", validate="many_to_one")\nprint(joined.loc[joined["name"] == "Ben", "room"].iloc[0])','The output is B. Ben\'s blue team matches room B in the second table.','Find Cara\'s room.','Duplicate keys can multiply rows; validate the relationship you expect.','What key joins the tables?','team','score'),
('Read a tiny CSV','CSV stores rows as text separated by commas. read_csv can read it from a file or, here, an in-memory text buffer.','from io import StringIO\nsmall = pd.read_csv(StringIO("item,count\\nbook,2\\npen,3\\n"))\nprint(small["count"].sum())','The result is 5. The two count values are added after pandas reads the text as a table.','Add a row for ruler,1.','Check inferred data types and missing values after reading a real file.','What is the total count?','5','23'),
('Copy a selected table before changing it','Make your intended copy explicit when you want to edit a subset independently. This keeps the original table easier to reason about.','selected = df.loc[df["team"] == "red"].copy()\nselected["score"] = 0\nprint(df["score"].tolist())','The original scores remain [8, 6, 9]. The change applies to the copied subset.','Print selected["score"] too.','Avoid chained assignment; use clear loc assignment or an explicit copy.','Which table is changed?','The selected copy','The original df'),
])

library('matplotlib','Matplotlib draws charts from data. Learn figures, axes, labels and honest chart choices using small fictional examples that you can explain without advanced maths.','Examples need Python and Matplotlib. Run them in a local notebook or suitable Python environment. Charts are described here; the website playground does not run Python.','https://matplotlib.org/stable/users/explain/quick_start.html','import matplotlib.pyplot as plt\n\n',[
('Figures and axes','A figure is the whole chart image. An Axes object is an area inside it where data is drawn.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.plot([1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 3])\nplt.show()','The line passes through the three given points. fig refers to the image; ax refers to its plotting area.','Draw the three points on paper first.','Matplotlib uses Axes as an object name; it is different from one single x or y axis.','What is fig?','The whole figure','Only the first data point'),
('Label the meaning','Axis labels explain what values mean and which units they use. A title tells the reader the chart\'s topic.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.plot([1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 3])\nax.set_xlabel("Day")\nax.set_ylabel("Books read")\nax.set_title("Reading this week")\nplt.show()','The chart now explains both axes. A reader does not need to guess whether 4 means books, minutes or people.','Change the data to minutes and update the label.','A chart without units can be misleading even if its points are correct.','Where should units be explained?','In clear labels','Only in the file name'),
('Compare categories with bars','A bar chart compares categories. Bars usually start from zero so their lengths fairly represent the values.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.bar(["Art", "Music", "Sport"], [3, 5, 4])\nax.set_ylabel("Votes")\nplt.show()','Music has the tallest bar because it has 5 votes.','Add a fourth activity with 2 votes.','Cutting off the zero baseline can exaggerate bar differences.','Which activity has the most votes?','Music','Art'),
('Use a scatter plot for pairs','A scatter plot shows paired measurements as separate points. It helps reveal a relationship without connecting every point.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.scatter([1, 2, 3], [4, 3, 5])\nax.set_xlabel("Practice sessions")\nax.set_ylabel("Points")\nplt.show()','The three points show three paired observations. A pattern alone would not prove one value causes the other.','Add a point at (4, 6).','Do not claim causation from a simple visual relationship.','What does each point represent?','One pair of measurements','The mean of every measurement'),
('Histograms group numeric values','A histogram groups numbers into bins and counts how many fall in each bin. A bin is an interval, not a category label.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.hist([1, 1, 2, 3, 3, 3], bins=[0.5, 1.5, 2.5, 3.5])\nax.set_xlabel("Books per learner")\nax.set_ylabel("Learners")\nplt.show()','The three bins have heights 2, 1 and 3. The explicit edges make the grouping clear.','Count the values in each bin by hand.','Changing bin widths changes the appearance of a histogram.','What does a bin group?','A range of numeric values','An arbitrary file name'),
('Legends identify series','A legend matches a line or marker to its label. It is useful when one chart has more than one series.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.plot([1, 2], [2, 3], label="Red team")\nax.plot([1, 2], [3, 4], label="Blue team")\nax.legend()\nplt.show()','The legend tells which line belongs to each team.','Use different line styles as well as labels.','Do not rely on color alone to distinguish important series.','What does a legend explain?','Which series is which','The password for the file'),
('Control visible limits carefully','Axis limits choose the part of the number range shown. Use them deliberately and tell readers when a narrow range matters.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.plot([1, 2, 3], [8, 9, 10])\nax.set_ylim(0, 12)\nplt.show()','The y-axis displays zero through 12, keeping the small differences in context.','Compare with limits 7 to 11 and describe how the impression changes.','A zoomed axis can make a small difference look huge.','What do y limits choose?','The visible y range','The true data values'),
('Place two plots in one figure','subplots can create several plotting areas. Give each area clear labels so readers can compare them correctly.','fig, axes = plt.subplots(1, 2)\naxes[0].bar(["A", "B"], [2, 3])\naxes[1].plot([1, 2], [3, 4])\nfig.tight_layout()\nplt.show()','The figure has one row and two plotting areas. The first is a bar chart; the second is a line chart.','Add a title to each plotting area.','Do not assume panels have identical scales unless you set or share them.','How many plotting areas are created?','2','1'),
('Grid lines support reading','A light grid can help readers estimate values. It should support the data rather than overpower it.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.plot([1, 2, 3], [2, 4, 3])\nax.grid(True, alpha=0.3)\nplt.show()','The grid is partly transparent, so the plotted line remains easy to see.','Compare the chart with the grid off.','Too many heavy lines make a chart harder to read.','Why use a light grid?','To help estimate values','To hide the data'),
('Save a figure to a file','savefig writes the figure to a file. Choose a format and size appropriate for how it will be used.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.bar(["Books", "Pens"], [2, 3])\nfig.savefig("practice-chart.png", dpi=150, bbox_inches="tight")\nplt.close(fig)','A PNG image is saved in the current working directory. Closing the figure releases its resources.','Use a new filename so you do not overwrite an important image.','Save the intended figure explicitly when several figures are open.','What does savefig produce?','An image file','A new dataset automatically'),
('Explain missing observations','Missing data should not be silently shown as a real zero. A gap can honestly show that no observation was recorded.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.plot([1, 2, 3], [2, float("nan"), 4], marker="o")\nplt.show()','The missing middle value breaks the line. It is not plotted as zero.','Explain the difference between no observation and an observed zero.','Filling missing data requires a justified choice, not a convenient visual shortcut.','Does a missing value mean zero?','No','Yes'),
('Review the story the chart tells','A chart should answer a clear question without exaggerating. Check title, units, scale and the underlying values before sharing.','fig, ax = plt.subplots()\nax.bar(["Monday", "Tuesday"], [4, 5])\nax.set_ylabel("Books borrowed")\nax.set_title("Fictional library example")\nax.set_ylim(0, 6)\nplt.show()','The title states the data is fictional. The zero-based scale shows a difference of one book.','Write one sentence that accurately describes the comparison.','Do not present practice numbers as collected real-world evidence.','How many more books are shown on Tuesday?','1','5'),
])
